WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.760
Welcome to Grade 4 English, Unit
4: Community and Communication.

2
00:00:05.320 --> 00:00:12.560
Unit 4 is about community and communication — the many
ways people share news, ideas and help with one another.

3
00:00:13.340 --> 00:00:18.160
You will read about a library service that
travels by cart, a young reporter called Maya,

4
00:00:18.600 --> 00:00:21.500
and a town meeting that opens a new community centre.

5
00:00:21.820 --> 00:00:27.360
You will learn words such as service, deliver,
knowledge, information, discover and priority,

6
00:00:27.820 --> 00:00:30.720
and use them to talk and write about your own community.

7
00:00:31.280 --> 00:00:38.540
By the end you will share a message clearly, just as
Amal does in this unit's story, “The Circular Plan”.

8
00:00:39.320 --> 00:00:44.580
In this short lecture, preview the key
ideas and prepare for the independent lesson.

9
00:00:45.240 --> 00:00:50.340
By the end of this unit, you will be able to talk
about services and communication in your community,

10
00:00:50.340 --> 00:00:52.460
using new Unit 4 words.

11
00:00:52.460 --> 00:00:57.440
You will also describe places and
things using There is and There are.

12
00:00:58.140 --> 00:01:04.780
You will also state facts about your town using the
present simple (“Postal workers deliver letters daily”).

13
00:01:05.880 --> 00:01:07.740
Keep these goals in mind as you work.

14
00:01:08.660 --> 00:01:17.100
Your vocabulary work begins with these words: careless,
useless, agreement, movement, payment, additionally.

15
00:01:18.060 --> 00:01:23.120
Listen to each pronunciation in the
vocabulary lab, study its meaning in context,

16
00:01:23.120 --> 00:01:26.740
and use the word accurately in
your own discussion and writing.

17
00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:33.420
The reading sequence includes The Library
That Came to Us and Maya the Young Reporter.

18
00:01:34.300 --> 00:01:35.080
Read actively.

19
00:01:35.760 --> 00:01:40.280
Identify each text's central ideas,
notice viewpoint and reliability,

20
00:01:40.280 --> 00:01:44.320
and support every inference with
relevant evidence from the text.

21
00:01:45.200 --> 00:01:49.320
The first language focus is Countable and Uncountable Nouns.

22
00:01:49.980 --> 00:01:57.560
Some nouns can be counted one by one — one book, two
books, three books — and we call these countable nouns.

23
00:01:58.240 --> 00:02:02.960
Other nouns cannot be split into separate
items, such as water, rice or information,

24
00:02:03.720 --> 00:02:05.560
and we call these uncountable nouns.

25
00:02:06.500 --> 00:02:12.200
Knowing the difference tells you whether to reach
for many or for much, so your sentences stay accurate.

26
00:02:12.860 --> 00:02:19.100
Notice the structure in the model examples, then apply
it deliberately in connected sentences and paragraphs.

27
00:02:20.420 --> 00:02:26.540
In speaking, you will complete Speaking
1: Describe a Service in Your Community.

28
00:02:26.870 --> 00:02:31.300
Organise your ideas, use evidence
and respond clearly to questions.

29
00:02:32.060 --> 00:02:35.360
In writing, you will plan, draft, check and revise.

30
00:02:36.120 --> 00:02:39.140
Use the success criteria before submitting your work.

31
00:02:39.680 --> 00:02:40.800
You are ready to begin.

32
00:02:40.800 --> 00:02:47.560
Start with the vocabulary lab, continue through
reading and comprehension, apply the grammar focus,

33
00:02:47.560 --> 00:02:50.420
and complete the speaking and writing practices.

34
00:02:51.300 --> 00:02:58.180
Use the AI tutor for hints and feedback any time, and
bring your questions to a live session too if you join one.
